microtun-firmware-signer for Debian
====================================

The package intentionally installs the systemd unit disabled and does not start
it during installation. The installed /etc/microtun-firmware-signer/config.toml
is an example configuration and must be reviewed before first use.

The package creates the dedicated microtun-firmware-signer system user and group,
and a separate microtun-signer-operators group, during configuration. If an
encrypted signing key is stored under /etc/microtun-firmware-signer, a typical
secure ownership/mode is:

  root:microtun-firmware-signer 0640

Do not add operators to the microtun-firmware-signer group: that group can read
the encrypted key file and the configuration. The application enforces that
key files are regular files (not symlinks), are owned by root or the service
user, are not group-writable, and are not accessible by other users.

Keys must be encrypted with PBES2/AES-256 and a strong KDF; weaker files are
refused at startup. For example:

  openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v2 aes-256-cbc -scrypt \
    -scrypt_N 16384 -scrypt_r 8 -scrypt_p 8 \
    -in plain-key.pem -out firmware-signing-key.encrypted.pem

(PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 with at least 600000 iterations is also accepted:
-v2prf hmacWithSHA256 -iter 600000.)

Signing-key passphrases are intentionally not systemd credentials. Every active
key starts locked after service startup.

Operator access
---------------

Two groups are involved, and they must stay separate:

  microtun-firmware-signer   The service's own group. Can read the encrypted
                             key file and config.toml. Only the service user
                             belongs here.
  microtun-signer-operators  People who unlock keys. Can reach the admin
                             socket, nothing else.

The pieces that connect them are all shipped by this package:

  - postinst creates both groups (they are kept if the package is purged).
  - The unit sets SupplementaryGroups=microtun-signer-operators, so the service
    may hand its socket to that group, and RuntimeDirectoryMode=0751, so
    operators can reach the socket without being able to list the directory.
  - The example config sets Server.AdminSocketGroup = "microtun-signer-operators".
    The service creates /run/microtun-firmware-signer/admin.sock as
    microtun-firmware-signer:microtun-signer-operators, mode 0660.

To grant a person operator rights:

  adduser alice microtun-signer-operators

Group membership only applies to new login sessions, so alice must log in
again (or run `newgrp microtun-signer-operators`) before it takes effect. Then:

  microtun-firmware-signer unlock --key-id microtun-firmware-prod

To confirm the setup on a running service:

  stat -c '%U:%G %a' /run/microtun-firmware-signer/admin.sock
    -> microtun-firmware-signer:microtun-signer-operators 660
  stat -c '%a' /run/microtun-firmware-signer
    -> 751
  getent group microtun-firmware-signer
    -> the last field (members) should be empty

To withdraw access: `deluser alice microtun-signer-operators`.

The unlock subcommand uses the default socket path and does not read the service
configuration unless --config is given explicitly, so operators need no access
to /etc/microtun-firmware-signer. Human GitHub OAuth sessions exist only in
process memory, so restarting the service invalidates them all (and relocks the
signing keys):

  systemctl restart microtun-firmware-signer.service
  microtun-firmware-signer unlock --key-id microtun-firmware-prod

The unlock subcommand reads the passphrase directly from the terminal with hidden
input in Rust and never accepts the key passphrase as a command-line argument or
environment variable. The service decrypts one key at a time, on a worker thread
separate from request handling, so an unlock does not stall signing requests.

GitHub OAuth client secret
--------------------------

The GitHub OAuth client secret is read only from a systemd credential; it is
not accepted from environment variables or arbitrary file paths. The credential
name matches GitHub.OAuthClientSecretCredential in the example configuration:

  LoadCredentialEncrypted=github-oauth-client-secret:/etc/credstore.encrypted/microtun-github-oauth-client-secret

Deployment-specific credential mappings should normally be placed in a systemd
drop-in instead of editing the packaged unit, for example:

  /etc/systemd/system/microtun-firmware-signer.service.d/credentials.conf

To create the encrypted credential from a file containing the secret:

  systemd-creds encrypt --name=github-oauth-client-secret \
    secret.txt /etc/credstore.encrypted/microtun-github-oauth-client-secret

After configuring the TOML, signing key file, and OAuth systemd credential:

  systemctl daemon-reload
  systemctl enable --now microtun-firmware-signer.service
  microtun-firmware-signer unlock --key-id microtun-firmware-prod
  systemctl status microtun-firmware-signer.service

The package installs /usr/bin/microtun-firmware-signer. The same binary provides
the `unlock` management subcommand and the `sign` public-API client subcommand.
